 "standard_code": "A-REI.B.4",
 "standard_url": "https://www.thecorestandards.org/Math/Content/HSA/REI/B/4/",
 "standard_text": "Solve quadratic equations by inspection (e.g., for x² = 49), taking square roots, completing the square, the quadratic formula and factoring, as appropriate to the initial form of the equation.",
 "meta_desc": "Free Algebra 1 worksheets on solving quadratic equations (A-REI.B.4b): the zero product property, solving by factoring, setting the equation to zero, solving by square roots and quadratic word problems. 100 questions with an answer key.",
 "key_big": "Make one side 0, factor, then set each factor equal to 0.",
 "key_sub": "x² = k has two solutions, ±√k. Reject answers that make no sense in the situation.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>The <b>zero product property</b>: if <i>A</i> · <i>B</i> = 0, then <i>A</i> = 0 or <i>B</i> = 0. To solve a quadratic by factoring:</p><ol><li>Move every term to one side so the other side is <b>0</b>.</li><li>Factor (take out the GCF first).</li><li>Set each factor equal to 0 and solve.</li></ol><p>If there is no <i>x</i>-term, take <b>square roots</b>: <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> = 49 gives <i>x</i> = ±7. In real situations, reject answers that make no sense, such as a negative length.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Solve <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + 2<i>x</i> = 15.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'><i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + 2<i>x</i> − 15 = 0.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>(<i>x</i> + 5)(<i>x</i> − 3) = 0.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>x</i> = −5 or <i>x</i> = 3</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Solve 3<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 12<i>x</i> = 0.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>3<i>x</i>(<i>x</i> − 4) = 0.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>x</i> = 0 or <i>x</i> = 4</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Solve (<i>x</i> − 2)<sup>2</sup> = 25.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'><i>x</i> − 2 = ±5.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>x</i> = 7 or <i>x</i> = −3</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Keeping the sign from the factor: <i>x</i> − 3 = 0 gives <i>x</i> = 3, not −3.</li><li>Dividing by <i>x</i> and losing the solution <i>x</i> = 0.</li><li>Factoring before the other side is 0: (<i>x</i> − 2)(<i>x</i> + 3) = 6 does not mean <i>x</i> − 2 = 6.</li><li>Forgetting the negative root: <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> = 49 has two solutions, 7 and −7.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What is the zero product property?",
   "a": "If the product of two factors is 0, at least one of the factors must be 0. So (x − 3)(x + 5) = 0 means x = 3 or x = −5."
  },
  {
   "q": "Why must one side be zero before factoring?",
   "a": "The zero product property only works for a product equal to 0. A product of 6 does not tell you what each factor is."
  },
  {
   "q": "When should I solve by square roots?",
   "a": "When there is no x-term, as in x² = 49 or (x − 2)² = 25. Remember both the positive and negative roots."
  },
  {
   "q": "Why do word problems sometimes have only one answer?",
   "a": "A quadratic often gives two solutions, but one may not make sense, such as a negative length or time."
  }
 ],
